1 DIFFERENCES

   Compares two files record by record and lists the records that
   differ.

   Format

     DIFFERENCES input1-filespec [input2-filespec]

2 Parameters

 input1-filespec

   The first file, the master: one file, with no wildcards.  A version
   left out is the newest.

 input2-filespec

   The second file, the revision.  The parts it leaves out are the
   first file's, but for the version: DIFFERENCES A.TXT .OLD compares
   A.TXT with the newest A.OLD.

   With no second file, the first is compared with its next lower
   version: DIFFERENCES A.TXT compares the newest A.TXT with the one
   before it.  When there is none, DIFFERENCES says so (NOLOWVER).

   At the terminal, DIFFERENCES with no files asks for them, "_File 1:"
   then "_File 2:"; an empty reply to the second compares the first
   with its next lower version.

2 Description

   DIFFERENCES walks both files a record at a time.  Where the records
   stop agreeing, a difference section starts, and it ends where the
   files agree again for /MATCH records in a row (3 unless told
   otherwise), or where both files end.  Records that agree inside a
   section, fewer than /MATCH of them, are part of it.  Where the files
   could get back in step at more than one place, the section ends
   where it leaves the fewest records on its larger side, taking more
   of the first file's when the sides would be the same.

   Each section is listed, then the number of sections and of
   difference records (a section counts as many as its larger side
   has), and last the command, written out with the qualifiers it
   used.

   In the default, merged, listing a section is

     ************
     File DUA1:[WORK]FOO.TXT;2
         3   line 3
     ******
     File DUA1:[WORK]FOO.TXT;1
         3   extra cat
         4   extra dog
         5   line 3
     ************

   each file's records of the section, and after them as many records
   that match as /MERGED says (1).  A record's line is its number, three
   blanks, and its text, cut at the listing's width.

   The other forms: /SEPARATED lists each file's records of every
   section under one heading; /PARALLEL lists a section's records side
   by side, tabs expanded and characters that don't print shown as
   periods (or <CR>, <LF>, <VT>, <FF>); /CHANGE_BAR after a file lists
   that whole file, its changed records marked.  Several forms in one
   command are listed in this order, a blank line between them: the
   parallel listing, the merged one, the separated ones, the change
   bars.  When /MAXIMUM_DIFFERENCES stops the comparison, only the first
   is listed.  Files that are the same get no listing, only the counts.

   The status DIFFERENCES leaves is %X006C8009 (success) when the files
   are the same, %X006C8013 (informational) when they differ, and
   %X006C8018 (warning, MAXDIF) when /MAXIMUM_DIFFERENCES stopped it.

2 Restrictions

   This version of govax lists in ASCII only: /MODE=HEXADECIMAL and
   OCTAL, and /SLP, are accepted and shown in the command written out,
   but the listing is the ASCII one.  /IGNORE and /COMMENT_DELIMITER are
   checked and shown, but nothing is ignored yet.  VMS's /PAGE, /SEARCH,
   /EXACT, /HIGHLIGHT, and /WRAP are not available.

2 Qualifiers
/CHANGE_BAR

   /CHANGE_BAR[=(character[,[NO]NUMBER])]

   Follows a file: lists that whole file, each record that differs
   marked with character ("!" by default), after its number.  Where the
   other file has records this one lacks, the record after the place is
   marked.  NUMBER and NONUMBER override /[NO]NUMBER for this listing.
   It is a file's qualifier only: after the verb, DCL refuses it
   (IVQLOC).  Given alone, only the change bar listings are made.

/COMMENT_DELIMITER

   /COMMENT_DELIMITER[=(character[,...])]

   Names up to 32 characters that start a comment, for
   /IGNORE=COMMENTS, which it implies.  A character is given as itself
   (quoted to keep its case) or by one of these keywords: COLON, COMMA,
   EXCLAMATION, FORM_FEED, LEFT, RIGHT, SEMI_COLON, SLASH, SPACE, TAB.
   With none, the file type chooses.

/IGNORE

   /IGNORE=(keyword[,...])

   What to leave out of the comparison: BLANK_LINES, CASE, COMMENTS,
   FORM_FEEDS, HEADER[=n] (n header records, 2 by default),
   SPACING, TRAILING_SPACES.  And how the listing shows what is
   ignored: EDITED (left out; the default), EXACT (shown as it is),
   PRETTY (formatted).

/MATCH

   /MATCH=n

   How many records must agree in a row for the files to be in step
   again after a difference.  The default is 3.

/MAXIMUM_DIFFERENCES

   /MAXIMUM_DIFFERENCES=n

   Stops comparing at the section that brings the count of difference
   records to n.  That section lists only as many records of each file
   as the count had left, and no matching records after them; the
   listing ends as usual, and then DIFFERENCES warns:

     %DIF-W-MAXDIF, maximum number of difference records encountered - DIF aborted

   0 is no limit.

/MERGED

   /MERGED[=n]

   The merged listing (the default), each side of a section followed by
   n records that match (1 by default).  n is at most /MATCH: a larger
   value is lowered to it, with a warning (MRGGTMTCH).

/MODE

   /MODE=(radix[,...])

   The listing's radix: ASCII (the default), HEXADECIMAL, OCTAL, each a
   listing of its own.

/NUMBER

   /NUMBER (default)
   /NONUMBER

   Whether a record's line starts with its number.  Under /PARALLEL,
   /NONUMBER leaves the record numbers out of each section's rule.

/OUTPUT

   /OUTPUT[=filespec]

   Writes the listing to a file rather than to SYS$OUTPUT.  With no file
   named, or only a directory, the file is the first file's name with
   type .DIF in the default directory; a name with no type gets .DIF.

/PARALLEL

   /PARALLEL[=n]

   Lists each section's records side by side, followed by n records
   that match (0 by default; at most /MATCH, with PARGTMTCH as for
   /MERGED).  Each section starts with a rule naming both files' first
   record numbers; each side is a little less than half the width.

/SEPARATED

   /SEPARATED[=(MASTER,REVISION)]

   Lists each file's records of every section on their own, the first
   file's, then the second's, each under one heading; with one keyword,
   only that file's.

/SLP

   Writes, in place of a listing, an edit script for the SLP editor
   that turns the first file into the second.  Its file is the first
   file's name with type .DIF unless /OUTPUT names another.  It can't be
   given with another listing's qualifier (CONFLICT).

/WIDTH

   /WIDTH=n

   The listing's width, 132 by default; at least 12.  A line longer is
   cut, the counts and the command written out are broken with "-".

/WINDOW

   /WINDOW=n

   How far past a difference, in records, to look for the files to be
   in step again before moving on: with no place found within n
   records of either file, DIFFERENCES looks again a record further on
   in both, and so on.  By default the search goes to the ends of both
   files.  A block of records moved further than n is listed as part of
   a larger section.  n must be greater than 0.

2 Examples

   1.$ DIFFERENCES FOO.TXT FOO.TST

     Lists the records FOO.TXT and FOO.TST don't share.

   2.$ DIFFERENCES LOGIN.COM

     Compares the newest LOGIN.COM with the version before it.

   3.$ DIFFERENCES/OUTPUT/MATCH=1 [WORK]REPORT.LIS .OLD

     Compares REPORT.LIS with REPORT.OLD, both in [WORK], the files in
     step again at the first record they agree on, and writes the
     listing to REPORT.DIF in the default directory.
